# pack_unpack_5trit: the proofs, for the REAL 5-trits-per-byte target.
#
# pack_unpack_block5 is a FINITE CASE ANALYSIS, not an induction --
# Trit has exactly 3 constructors, so 5 nested matches (one per trit)
# produce exactly 3^5 = 243 leaf branches, one level deeper each than
# bend/pack_unpack/PROOF.bend's 3^2 = 9-leaf pack_unpack_pair (which
# this mirrors exactly, just for 5 trits instead of 2). In every leaf,
# all five of t4..t0 are concrete constructors, so pack5's Horner
# arithmetic (Nat.mul/Nat.add on literals) and unpack5's div/mod chain
# both fully compute down to closed Nat/Block5 terms with no symbolic
# reasoning needed -- both sides of the goal are then the same computed
# term, so {==} closes every branch, exactly as in pack_unpack_pair.
# This exhaustiveness is also what caught the real bug documented in
# docs/bend-proof.md: with the buggy digit order, this same 243-way
# check would fail on the very first branch where the block isn't a
# palindrome (see buggy_first_attempt_disproved.bend for that exact
# rejection, caught before this full proof was even attempted).
#
# This file was generated by a small script
# (see docs/bend-proof.md for why: 243 hand-typed branches is exactly
# the kind of place a manual transcription slip would be as plausible a
# bug as the packing logic itself) and then checked with `bend`, the
# same as every other proof in this repo -- the generation script is
# mechanical (a template applied 243 times), not part of what's being
# proven; what bend actually checks is the literal 728-line def below.

import Base
