# pack_unpack5: structural induction on the list of 5-trit groups, one
# group per step -- the exact same shape as bend/pack_unpack/PROOF.bend's
# pack_unpack over List<&2, Block2>, just with pack_unpack_block5 (above)
# standing in for pack_unpack_pair as the per-group lemma:
#
#   xs = Nil{}: pack5_list(Nil{}) = Nil{} = unpack5_list(Nil{}), so both
#   sides of the goal are already Nil{}: {==}.
#
#   xs = Con{p, rest} with p = B5{t4,t3,t2,t1,t0}: unfolding pack5_list
#   and unpack5_list by definition, the goal the checker actually holds
#   is
#     {Con{unpack5(pack5(t4,t3,t2,t1,t0)), unpack5_list(pack5_list(rest))}
#        == Con{B5{t4,t3,t2,t1,t0}, rest} : List<&2, Block5>}
#   Two rewrites close it, identically to pack_unpack's:
#     1. pack_unpack_block5(t4,t3,t2,t1,t0), flipped by Equal.sym (the
#        goal needs the stuck LHS term rewritten, and the lemma is
#        stated the other way round), rewrites the head of the Con on
#        the left down to B5{t4,t3,t2,t1,t0}.
#     2. The induction hypothesis Laws.pack_unpack5(rest) rewrites the
#        tail on the left down to rest.
#   Both sides are then literally Con{B5{t4,t3,t2,t1,t0},
#   unpack5_list(pack5_list(rest))}: {==}.
def Laws.pack_unpack5(xs):
  match xs:
    case Nil{}:
      {==}
    case Con{p, rest}:
      match p:
        case Q.B5{t4, t3, t2, t1, t0}:
          %Equal.sym(Q.Block5, Q.unpack5(Q.pack5(t4, t3, t2, t1, t0)), Q.B5{t4, t3, t2, t1, t0}, Laws.pack_unpack_block5(t4, t3, t2, t1, t0)) : {Con{_, Q.unpack5_list(Q.pack5_list(rest))} == Con{Q.B5{t4, t3, t2, t1, t0}, rest} : List<&2, Q.Block5>}
          %Laws.pack_unpack5(rest) : {Con{Q.B5{t4, t3, t2, t1, t0}, Q.unpack5_list(Q.pack5_list(rest))} == Con{Q.B5{t4, t3, t2, t1, t0}, _} : List<&2, Q.Block5>}
          {==}
